data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":15152,"item_id":"fc_0b9ca2ffc961a3af016ae49f7f1fd48191abd6b99ba8d47cb1","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"spec\":{\"format_version\":2,\"case_id\":\"wave-map-energy\",\"full_target\":\"A sphere-valued map cannot vanish outside a compact set. As approved by\\nthe user on 2026-10-23, interpret spatial compact support as compact support\\nof a perturbation from a **fixed unit vector**, locally uniformly in time.\\nThis is a disclosed nontrivial interpretation, not a claimed author-issued\\ncorrection.\\n\\nLet \\\\(n,m\\\\geq1\\\\) be integers and let\\n\\\\(u:\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\times(0,\\\\infty)\\\\to\\\\mathbb R^m\\\\) be jointly smooth.\\nFix \\\\(q\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^m\\\\) with \\\\(|q|=1\\\\). Assume:\\n\\n1. \\\\(|u(x,t)|=1\\\\) for all \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(t>0\\\\).\\n2. The acceleration \\\\(u_{tt}-\\\\Delta_xu\\\\) is normal to the sphere at\\n   \\\\(u\\\\): at each point there is a real scalar \\\\(a(x,t)\\\\) with\\n   \\\\(u_{tt}-\\\\Delta_xu=a u\\\\). No regularity of the selected scalar is\\n   assumed.\\n3. For every \\\\(0<s<t<\\\\infty\\\\), a compact set\\n   \\\\(C_{[s,t]}\\\\subset\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\) exists such that \\\\(u(x,r)=q\\\\) for\\n   \\\\(x\\\\notin C_{[s,t]}\\\\), \\\\(r\\\\in[s,t]\\\\).\\n\\nWith Euclidean norms and Lebesgue measure, define\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne(x,t)=|u_t(x,t)|^2+\\\\sum_{j=1}^n|\\\\partial_{x_j}u(x,t)|^2,\\n\\\\qquad E(t)=\\\\int_{\\\\mathbb R^n}e(x,t)\\\\,dx.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe spatial gradient square is the Frobenius square, and\\n\\\\(\\\\Delta_xu=\\\\sum_j\\\\partial_{x_jx_j}u\\\\) component by component.\\nProve integrability of \\\\(e(\\\\cdot,t)\\\\), differentiability of \\\\(E\\\\),\\nand \\\\(E'(t)=0\\\\) for every \\\\(t>0\\\\). No assertion at time zero is required.\",\"conclusion\":\"For every real t>0, e(·,t) is Lebesgue integrable on ℝ^n, E is differentiable at t, and E'(t)=0. In particular E is constant on (0,∞). No assertion at time zero is made.\",\"parameters\":[{\"symbol\":\"n\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"u\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m; the Euclidean spaces carry their standard dot products, norms, topology, and Lebesgue measures\"},{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"vector in ℝ^m\"}],\"hypotheses\":[{\"id\":\"H001\",\"statement\":\"n≥1 and m≥1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:25\"},{\"id\":\"H002\",\"statement\":\"u is jointly smooth on the open set ℝ^n × (0,∞).\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:25-26\"},{\"id\":\"H003\",\"statement\":\"|q|=1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:27\"},{\"id\":\"H004\",\"statement\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and every t>0, |u(x,t)|=1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:29\"},{\"id\":\"H005\",\"statement\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and every t>0 there exists a real scalar a such that u_tt(x,t)−Δu(x,t)=a u(x,t). No regularity of such scalars as a function of (x,t) is assumed.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:30-33\"},{\"id\":\"H006\",\"statement\":\"For every pair of real numbers 0<s<t<∞ there exists a compact set C⊆ℝ^n such that u(x,r)=q whenever x∉C and r∈[s,t].\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:34-36\"}],\"definitions\":[{\"id\":\"D001\",\"symbol\":\"u_t\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"u_t(x,t)=∂u(x,t)/∂t, component by component. Derivatives are the usual real derivatives; a derivative not existing is assigned the total derivative convention value zero. Under H002 these are classical derivatives.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\",\"m\",\"x\",\"t\"]},{\"id\":\"D002\",\"symbol\":\"u_tt\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"u_tt(x,t)=∂u_t(x,t)/∂t, component by component, with the same total derivative convention.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D001\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_t\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D003\",\"symbol\":\"u_j\",\"type\":\"family indexed by j∈{1,…,n} of maps ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"u_j(j,x,t)=∂_{x_j}u(x,t), the derivative along the jth standard coordinate direction, component by component with the total derivative convention. We also write u_j when the index j is explicitly fixed.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\",\"m\",\"j\",\"x\",\"t\"]},{\"id\":\"D004\",\"symbol\":\"u_jj\",\"type\":\"family indexed by j∈{1,…,n} of maps ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"u_jj(j,x,t)=∂_{x_j}u_j(j,x,t), with the total derivative convention. The notation u_jj with fixed j has this same meaning.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D003\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_j\",\"j\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D005\",\"symbol\":\"v_j\",\"type\":\"family indexed by j∈{1,…,n} of maps ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"v_j(j,x,t)=∂_{x_j}u_t(x,t), with the total derivative convention. With fixed j we abbreviate this by v_j.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D001\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_t\",\"j\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D006\",\"symbol\":\"Δu\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"Δu(x,t)=∑_{j=1}^n u_jj(j,x,t), the componentwise spatial Laplacian Δ_xu of the input.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D004\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_jj\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D007\",\"symbol\":\"e\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ\",\"definition\":\"e(x,t)=|u_t(x,t)|²+∑_{j=1}^n |u_j(j,x,t)|². Here |z|²=z·z is the Euclidean norm square on ℝ^m, so the spatial sum is exactly the Frobenius square |Du|². All integrals in the proof are Lebesgue integrals, and dx denotes standard volume on ℝ^n.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D001\",\"D003\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_t\",\"u_j\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D008\",\"symbol\":\"e_t\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ\",\"definition\":\"e_t(x,t)=∂e(x,t)/∂t, using the total derivative convention, whose classical existence and formula will be proved.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D007\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\"]},{\"id\":\"D009\",\"symbol\":\"E\",\"type\":\"map (0,∞) → ℝ\",\"definition\":\"For t>0, E(t)=∫_{ℝ^n}e(x,t) dx. This is the finite Lebesgue integral, used only after N002 has proved integrability at every positive time; no definition at time zero is required.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D007\"],\"requires\":[\"N002\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"n\",\"t\"]}],\"logic\":{\"classical\":true,\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\",\"implication_introduction\"]},\"pins\":{},\"inputs\":[]},\"graph\":{\"format_version\":2,\"case_id\":\"wave-map-energy\",\"spec_sha256\":\"controller-bound\",\"propositions\":[{\"id\":\"H001\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"H002\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\"]},{\"id\":\"H003\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"q\"]},{\"id\":\"H004\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\",\"x\",\"t\"]},{\"id\":\"H005\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[\"D002\",\"D006\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar, existentially quantified at each (x,t)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"u_tt\",\"Δu\",\"x\",\"t\",\"a\",\"n\"]},{\"id\":\"H006\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"s\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"subset of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"q\",\"n\",\"s\",\"t\",\"C\",\"x\",\"r\"]},{\"id\":\"M001\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A be a measurable space with measure μ and G a normed additive commutative group and real normed space. Let I be a type, l a countably generated filter on I, F:I→A→G, f:A→G and b:A→ℝ. If eventually along l each F(i) is μ-almost-everywhere strongly measurable, eventually along l |F(i)(x)|≤b(x) for μ-almost every x, b is μ-integrable, and for μ-almost every x the values F(i)(x) tend along l to f(x), then ∫F(i)dμ tends along l to ∫f dμ.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"measure on A\"},{\"symbol\":\"G\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group with real normed-space structure\"},{\"symbol\":\"I\",\"type\":\"type\"},{\"symbol\":\"l\",\"type\":\"filter on I\"},{\"symbol\":\"F\",\"type\":\"map I→A→G\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map A→G\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"map A→ℝ\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"element of A\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"μ\",\"G\",\"I\",\"l\",\"F\",\"f\",\"b\",\"i\",\"x\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.tendsto_integral_filter_of_dominated_convergence\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A has a measurable-space structure and μ is a measure; G is a normed additive commutative group with a real normed-space structure.\",\"l is countably generated.\",\"F(i) is μ-a.e. strongly measurable eventually along l.\",\"The norm bound |F(i)(x)|≤b(x) holds μ-a.e. eventually along l.\",\"b is μ-integrable.\",\"F(i)(x) tends along l to f(x) for μ-almost every x.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M002\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let V be a real normed additive commutative group with a normed-space structure over a nontrivially normed field K that is real-like or complex-like, and let W be a normed additive commutative group and normed K-space. For f:V→W, a set S⊆V, a real c, points p,r∈V and L:V→(V→L[K]W), assume f has Fréchet derivative L(z) within S at every z∈S, |L(z)|≤c for every z∈S, S is real convex, and p,r∈S. Then |f(r)−f(p)|≤c|r−p|.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"real normed space also a normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"nontrivially normed field with IsRCLikeNormedField structure\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map V→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"S\",\"type\":\"subset of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"p\",\"type\":\"point of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"point of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"L\",\"type\":\"map V→ continuous K-linear maps V→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"point of V\"}],\"objects\":[\"V\",\"K\",\"W\",\"f\",\"S\",\"c\",\"p\",\"r\",\"L\",\"z\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_hasFDerivWithin_le\",\"hypotheses\":[\"V is a real normed space and a normed K-space; K is a nontrivially normed field with IsRCLikeNormedField; W is a normed K-space.\",\"f has Fréchet derivative L(z) within S at every z∈S.\",\"For every z∈S, the operator norm |L(z)|≤c.\",\"S is convex over ℝ.\",\"p belongs to S.\",\"r belongs to S.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M003\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let W be a complete real normed space and f:ℝ→W, with real endpoints a,b. If f is differentiable at every point of the unordered closed interval with endpoints a,b and deriv f is interval-integrable with respect to real Lebesgue measure from a to b, then the oriented interval integral ∫_a^b deriv f(z) dz equals f(b)−f(a).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"complete real normed space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"W\",\"f\",\"a\",\"b\",\"z\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"intervalIntegral.integral_deriv_eq_sub\",\"hypotheses\":[\"W is a normed additive commutative group, real normed space and complete space.\",\"f is differentiable at every point of the unordered closed interval between a and b.\",\"deriv f is Lebesgue interval-integrable from a to b.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M005\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let X be a topological space with a measurable-space structure for which open sets are measurable, let μ be a measure finite on compact sets, and let W be a normed additive commutative group. Every continuous f:X→W having compact support (the closure of {x:f(x)≠0} is compact) is μ-integrable.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"X\",\"type\":\"topological and measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"measure on X\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map X→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of X\"}],\"objects\":[\"X\",\"μ\",\"W\",\"f\",\"x\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Continuous.integrable_of_hasCompactSupport\",\"hypotheses\":[\"X is topological and measurable with open sets measurable; μ is finite on compact sets; W is a normed additive commutative group.\",\"f is continuous.\",\"The closure of the nonzero set of f is compact.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M006\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let K be an RCLike field, W an inner product space over K (and normed additive commutative group) also carrying a real normed-space structure, and f,g:ℝ→W. If f and g are real differentiable at a real point z, then deriv(t↦⟨f(t),g(t)⟩_K)(z)=⟨f(z),deriv g(z)⟩_K+⟨deriv f(z),g(z)⟩_K.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"RCLike field\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group, inner product space over K, and real normed space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"g\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"W\",\"f\",\"g\",\"z\",\"t\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"deriv_inner_apply\",\"hypotheses\":[\"K is RCLike; W has the stated normed group, K-inner-product and real normed-space structures.\",\"f is real differentiable at z.\",\"g is real differentiable at z.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M007\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let K be a nontrivially normed field and V,W normed additive commutative groups and normed K-spaces. Let f:V→W, z∈V, and k∈ℕ∞ω. If f is ContDiffAt K k at z and minSmoothness K 2≤k, then its second Fréchet derivative at z is symmetric: for every v,w∈V, D(Df)(z)(v)(w)=D(Df)(z)(w)(v). Here ContDiffAt is the native local smoothness predicate and minSmoothness K 2 is 2 for real-like/complex-like fields and ω otherwise.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"nontrivially normed field\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map V→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"point of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"k\",\"type\":\"smoothness index in ℕ∞ω\"},{\"symbol\":\"v\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"},{\"symbol\":\"w\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"}],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"V\",\"W\",\"f\",\"z\",\"k\",\"v\",\"w\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"ContDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt\",\"hypotheses\":[\"K is a nontrivially normed field; V and W are normed additive commutative groups and normed K-spaces.\",\"f is ContDiffAt K k at z.\",\"minSmoothness K 2≤k.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M008\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let X be a topological space, W a seminormed additive group, S⊆X compact, and f:X→W continuous on S. There exists a real c such that |f(x)|≤c for every x∈S.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"X\",\"type\":\"topological space\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"seminormed additive group\"},{\"symbol\":\"S\",\"type\":\"subset of X\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map X→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of X\"}],\"objects\":[\"X\",\"W\",\"S\",\"f\",\"c\",\"x\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"IsCompact.exists_bound_of_continuousOn\",\"hypotheses\":[\"X is a topological space and W is a seminormed additive group.\",\"S is compact.\",\"f is continuous on S.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M009\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For every natural k, family A_i of measure spaces indexed by i∈Fin(k+1), each with sigma-finite volume, and index i∈Fin(k+1), the measurable equivalence which sends a tuple x to (x_i,(x_{i.succAbove(j)})_{j∈Fin k}) preserves volume from the finite product of the A_i to A_i times the product of the remaining spaces. Here i.succAbove lists the remaining indices in order.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"k\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"family of measure spaces indexed by Fin(k+1)\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of Fin(k+1)\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"tuple in the product of A\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"element of Fin k\"}],\"objects\":[\"k\",\"A\",\"i\",\"x\",\"j\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.volume_preserving_piFinSuccAbove\",\"hypotheses\":[\"Each A_i is a measure space.\",\"Each coordinate volume is sigma-finite.\",\"i is an index in Fin(k+1).\"]}},{\"id\":\"M010\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A,B be measurable spaces with s-finite measures μ,ν respectively, and W a normed additive commutative group and real normed space. For every f:A×B→W integrable with respect to μ×ν, ∫_{A×B}f d(μ×ν)=∫_B(∫_A f(x,y)dμ(x))dν(y).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"s-finite measure on A\"},{\"symbol\":\"ν\",\"type\":\"s-finite measure on B\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and real normed space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map A×B→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"element of A\"},{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"element of B\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"B\",\"μ\",\"ν\",\"W\",\"f\",\"x\",\"y\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.integral_prod_symm\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A,B are measurable spaces with s-finite measures μ,ν; W is a normed additive commutative group and real normed space.\",\"f is integrable for the product measure μ×ν.\"]}},{\"id\":\"N001\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and t>0, u(x,t)·u_t(x,t)=0 and u_t(x,t)·(u_tt(x,t)−Δu(x,t))=0. Moreover u·u_tt=−|u_t|² and u·u_jj(j,x,t)=−|u_j(j,x,t)|² for every j∈{1,…,n}. Every scalar a satisfying u_tt−Δu=a u at (x,t) necessarily equals ∑_{j=1}^n|u_j(j,x,t)|²−|u_t(x,t)|²; thus the explicit sphere wave-map equation is a consequence, not an extra assumption.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D006\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"Δu\",\"x\",\"t\",\"j\",\"a\",\"n\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:52-68; tangency and the optional explicit PDE calculation are retained.\"},{\"id\":\"N002\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"n≥1 and m≥1. The map u and all its partial derivatives are smooth on ℝ^n×(0,∞); in particular ∂_t u_j(j,x,t)=v_j(j,x,t) and e_t(x,t)=2u_t(x,t)·u_tt(x,t)+2∑_{j=1}^n u_j(j,x,t)·v_j(j,x,t). For every t0>0, let J=[t0/2,3t0/2]. There exist a compact C⊆ℝ^n and R>0 such that C⊆(−R,R)^n, u(x,r)=q for x∉C and r∈J, and every positive-order derivative of u vanishes for x∉C and r∈int J. Put Q=[−R,R]^n, whose Lebesgue measure is (2R)^n. For every r∈int J the following scalar spatial functions are continuous, zero outside C, and Lebesgue integrable: e(·,r), e_t(·,r), u_t(·,r)·u_tt(·,r), and, for each j, u_j(j,·,r)·v_j(j,·,r), u_t(·,r)·u_jj(j,·,r), u_t(·,r)·u_j(j,·,r), and ∂_{x_j}(u_t(·,r)·u_j(j,·,r)). Their absolute integrals are finite, each bounded by a finite supremum on Q times (2R)^n. In particular e(·,t) is integrable for every t>0.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"J\",\"type\":\"closed time interval\"},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real number, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed spatial box\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real time\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"u\",\"q\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"Δu\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"x\",\"t\",\"t0\",\"J\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"r\",\"j\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:70-94; common support, integrability, and the differentiated density.\"},{\"id\":\"N003\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every t0>0 the finite integral E is differentiable at t0 and E'(t0)=∫_{ℝ^n}e_t(x,t0) dx.\",\"definitions\":[\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"E\",\"e_t\",\"t0\",\"x\",\"n\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:87-117; bounded difference quotients and dominated convergence.\"},{\"id\":\"N004\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every t0>0 and j∈{1,…,n}, both integrands below are Lebesgue integrable and ∫_{ℝ^n}u_j(j,x,t0)·v_j(j,x,t0) dx=−∫_{ℝ^n}u_t(x,t0)·u_jj(j,x,t0) dx.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_j\",\"v_j\",\"u_t\",\"u_jj\",\"t0\",\"j\",\"x\",\"n\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:119-142; coordinatewise FTC and Fubini integration by parts.\"},{\"id\":\"P\",\"kind\":\"target\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"E\",\"n\",\"t\"]}],\"inferences\":[{\"id\":\"R001\",\"inputs\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"H005\",\"M006\"],\"output\":\"N001\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D006\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R001/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix an arbitrary spatial point x.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R001/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix an arbitrary t>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar\",\"scope\":\"R001/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose a scalar a given by H005 at (x,t), so u_tt(x,t)−Δu(x,t)=a u(x,t).\",\"from_inputs\":[\"H005\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"u\",\"u_tt\",\"Δu\"]},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\",\"scope\":\"R001/S003 spatial calculation\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"For the spatial second-derivative identity, fix any j∈{1,…,n}.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix arbitrary x∈ℝ^n and t>0. H004 gives u·u=1 on the whole domain. The time section is differentiable near t by H002. Applying M006 with both functions equal to this section gives 0=∂_t(u·u)=2u·u_t, hence u·u_t=0. Whenever a source is formulated for a function on all of ℝ, a time section may be set to zero for nonpositive times; it agrees with the given section on a neighborhood of t, so its derivative at t is unchanged. This convention is only for applying sources and does not define u at time zero.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"For every spatial point y∈ℝ^n and every positive time r, u(y,r)·u_t(y,r)=0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"u_t\",\"y\",\"r\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose a from H005 at (x,t). Dot its identity with u_t. F001 at (x,t) and symmetry of the Euclidean dot product give u_t·(u_tt−Δu)=a(u_t·u)=0. Only this pointwise choice is used; a is never differentiated.\",\"uses\":[\"H005\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"a\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"Δu\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"u_t(x,t)·(u_tt(x,t)−Δu(x,t))=0.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"Δu\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Retain also the supplied explicit equation calculation. Since F001 is an identity on an open time interval, M006 applied to the time sections of u and u_t gives 0=∂_t(u·u_t)=|u_t|²+u·u_tt, so u·u_tt=−|u_t|². For any fixed j, apply M006 first to u with itself along a spatial coordinate line, obtaining u·u_j=0 from H004, and then to u and u_j along that line, obtaining u·u_jj=−|u_j|². All sections and their derivatives are differentiable by H002. Dot the equation defining a with u. Since u·u=1, a=u·u_tt−∑_j u·u_jj=∑_j|u_j|²−|u_t|². The last computation works for every scalar satisfying the normality equation, and does not give it any assumed regularity. Together with F001 and F002, universal introduction in x,t and the arbitrary spatial index proves N001.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"j\",\"a\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"Δu\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"id\":\"A001\",\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, W=ℝ^m; f=g is the time section r↦u(x,r), extended by zero for r≤0; z=t.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The real field and Euclidean ℝ^m have the required real inner-product and normed structures.\",\"H002 on the open positive-time domain gives differentiability of f at t>0.\",\"g=f, so the same differentiability holds.\"]},{\"id\":\"A002\",\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, W=ℝ^m. First use f(r)=u(x,r), g(r)=u_t(x,r), z=t; also use along each spatial line first f=g=u and then f=u,g=u_j, at the coordinate of x.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"All these sections take values in Euclidean ℝ^m, a real inner-product and normed space.\",\"H002 gives differentiability of each section of u at the displayed point.\",\"H002 gives differentiability of u_t or u_j, and also of u when g=u.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R002\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"H006\",\"M005\",\"M006\",\"M007\",\"M008\"],\"output\":\"N002\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix any t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"J\",\"type\":\"closed interval in ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set J=[t0/2,3t0/2].\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose C with the common support property in F001.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"J\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose R>0 with C⊆(−R,R)^n from F002.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed box in ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Put Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"n\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"H001 supplies n,m≥1. Fix t0>0 and J=[t0/2,3t0/2]. The endpoints satisfy 0<t0/2<3t0/2, so H006 gives a compact spatial set common to all times in J. Separately, H002 ensures that all partial derivatives of u are smooth on the open domain. M007 over ℝ at each point, applied to the spatial and time coordinate vectors, gives ∂_t u_j=v_j. The input is locally smooth at that point, so it is in particular C² there; if a total ambient map is needed, extend u by zero off the positive-time domain, which leaves it smooth on a neighborhood of the point. Differentiating each squared norm using M006, and the finite-sum differentiation rule, therefore gives e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2∑_j u_j·v_j throughout the domain. These formulas also show e and e_t are smooth.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"H006\",\"M006\",\"M007\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"t0\",\"J\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"v_j\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"q\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"There exists a compact C0⊆ℝ^n such that u(y,r)=q for every y∉C0 and r∈J.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"C0\",\"type\":\"subset of ℝ^n, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real time in J\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"C0\",\"y\",\"r\",\"J\",\"u\",\"q\"],\"assumptions\":[]},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"statement\":\"All partial derivatives of u are smooth on the open positive-time domain; ∂_t u_j=v_j and e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2∑_{j=1}^n u_j·v_j at every point. The functions e and e_t are smooth there.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"u_j\",\"v_j\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose C from F001. Apply M008 on C to the identity map into Euclidean ℝ^n. It gives a finite real bound on |x| for x∈C. Increase this bound, if necessary, to a positive R larger than it. Since |x_j|≤|x|, C⊆(−R,R)^n. Thus such a positive R exists, even if C is empty.\",\"uses\":[\"M008\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"There exists R0>0 such that C⊆(−R0,R0)^n.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"R0\",\"type\":\"real number, existential\"}],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"n\",\"R0\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Choose R from F002 and set Q=[−R,R]^n. This closed box is compact, measurable, and has finite Lebesgue measure (2R)^n. The compact set C is closed in ℝ^n. On the open set (ℝ^n\\\\C)×int J, F001 says u=q. At every point of this open set u is constant in a neighborhood; differentiating the constant on that neighborhood, successively in each coordinate, gives zero for every positive-order derivative. Consequently at any r∈int J all the functions listed in N002 vanish outside C: every summand contains such a derivative. For the last function, the product rule expands it into v_j·u_j+u_t·u_jj, both of which vanish there. Continuity and smoothness follow from F003 and smoothness of the dot product.\",\"uses\":[],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\",\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"J\",\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"e\",\"e_t\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F004\",\"statement\":\"C⊆int Q, Q is compact and measurable with finite Lebesgue measure (2R)^n, and for every r∈int J all the scalar spatial functions listed in N002 are continuous and vanish outside C. In particular their nonzero sets have closures contained in the compact set C.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"time in int J\"}],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"Q\",\"R\",\"n\",\"J\",\"r\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"e\",\"e_t\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S004\",\"text\":\"For every r∈int J apply M005 to each continuous scalar function in F004, on ℝ^n with Lebesgue measure. Its support closure is a closed subset of compact C, hence compact; Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets. Each is therefore integrable. Equivalently its norm is bounded on Q by M008 and it vanishes outside Q, so its absolute integral is at most that finite bound times |Q|=(2R)^n. This also supplies the stated finiteness estimate for all terms. Since t0 belongs to int J, e(·,t0) is integrable. As t0 was arbitrary, this holds at every positive time. Together with F003 and F004 this proves the complete statement N002, including the common support witnesses and all derivative-product integrability assertions.\",\"uses\":[\"M005\",\"M008\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F003\",\"F004\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"C\",\"Q\",\"R\",\"J\",\"t0\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ,W=ℝ^m; for each spatial point take f=g equal to the time section of u_t or of u_j, respectively.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean ℝ^m has the required inner-product and real normed-space structures.\",\"Each section f is differentiable by H002 at every positive time.\",\"g=f has the same differentiability.\"]},{\"input\":\"M007\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ,V=ℝ^n×ℝ,W=ℝ^m,f the zero extension of u, z any point of the positive-time domain,k=2; evaluate symmetry on the jth spatial coordinate and the time coordinate.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The real field and finite-dimensional Euclidean domain and codomain have the required normed-space structures.\",\"H002 gives local C² regularity at z since the time coordinate of z is positive; the extension agrees with u on an open neighborhood of z.\",\"minSmoothness ℝ 2=2≤2.\"]},{\"id\":\"A001\",\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n,W=ℝ^n,S=C,f the identity.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean space is topological and normed.\",\"C is compact by the witness chosen from F001.\",\"The identity is continuous on C.\"]},{\"input\":\"M005\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n,μ=Lebesgue volume,W=ℝ,f successively each scalar section listed in F004, at each r∈int J.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The Borel/Lebesgue measurable structure makes open sets measurable; Euclidean Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets; ℝ is a normed additive commutative group.\",\"F004 proves continuity of every section.\",\"F004 places the closure of each nonzero set in compact C; it is closed and hence compact.\"]},{\"id\":\"A002\",\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n,W=ℝ,S=Q,f each scalar section in F004.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The domain is Euclidean and the codomain ℝ is seminormed.\",\"F004 proves Q compact.\",\"F004 proves each section continuous, in particular on Q.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R003\",\"inputs\":[\"N002\",\"M001\",\"M002\",\"M008\"],\"output\":\"N003\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose C from N002 at t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose the corresponding R from N002 so C⊆(−R,R)^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"n\",\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed spatial box\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"η\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set η=t0/4.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"I\",\"type\":\"closed time interval\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set I=[t0−η,t0+η].\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"η\"]},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"nonnegative real number\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose B≥0 satisfying F001.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"e_t\",\"Q\",\"I\"]},{\"symbol\":\"F\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"For 0<|h|<η define F(h)(x)=(e(x,t0+h)−e(x,t0))/h; for other h define F(h)(x)=0. This is total, since every t0+h in the first case is positive.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define b(x)=B 1_Q(x), where 1_Q equals one on Q and zero off Q.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"B\",\"Q\",\"n\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0 and choose C,R from N002. Put Q=[−R,R]^n, η=t0/4 and I=[t0−η,t0+η]. The elementary endpoint inequalities place I strictly inside (t0/2,3t0/2). N002 makes E a finite integral at every positive time, so its guarded definition is now available. Its density and e_t are continuous on the compact set Q×I. M008 applied there to e_t gives a finite bound; replacing it by its maximum with zero gives a B≥0 with |e_t(x,r)|≤B on Q×I.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M008\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"η\",\"I\",\"n\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"E\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"There exists B0≥0 such that |e_t(x,r)|≤B0 for every x∈Q and r∈I.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"B0\",\"type\":\"real number, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of Q\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"time in I\"}],\"objects\":[\"B0\",\"e_t\",\"x\",\"r\",\"Q\",\"I\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose B as in F001 and define F and b as in the local declarations. For any x∈Q, the scalar time section r↦e(x,r) is differentiable on I by N002, with derivative e_t(x,r). Apply M002 on the convex interval I, with derivative the linear map multiplying by e_t(x,r), whose operator norm is |e_t(x,r)|≤B. For 0<|h|<η both t0 and t0+h lie in I, so |e(x,t0+h)−e(x,t0)|≤B|h|. Division by |h| gives |F(h)(x)|≤B. Outside Q, N002 gives both densities zero, since both times lie inside the common time slab. Hence |F(h)(x)|≤b(x) on all of ℝ^n. The function b is integrable: Q is measurable of finite measure (2R)^n and ∫|b|=B(2R)^n<∞. For each such h, F(h) is continuous and integrable, by N002's integrability at its two times and linearity. At each x, the definition of e_t as the classical derivative gives F(h)(x)→e_t(x,t0) as h→0 through nonzero values.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"B\",\"F\",\"b\",\"n\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"I\",\"Q\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"b is Lebesgue integrable; for all real h with 0<|h|<η, F(h) is continuous and integrable and |F(h)(x)|≤b(x) for every x∈ℝ^n. For every x∈ℝ^n, F(h)(x) tends to e_t(x,t0) as h→0 through h≠0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"h\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"b\",\"F\",\"h\",\"x\",\"η\",\"e_t\",\"t0\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Use M001 with parameter filter the punctured neighborhood filter h→0, functions F(h), limit e_t(·,t0), and bound b. This filter is countably generated: the punctured intervals 0<|h|<1/k, with positive integer k, form a countable neighborhood basis. F002 supplies continuity, hence a.e. strong measurability, eventually, the everywhere dominating bound, integrability of b, and pointwise convergence. Therefore ∫F(h)→∫e_t(·,t0). For 0<|h|<η, the integrability assertions of N002 give by linearity ∫F(h)=(E(t0+h)−E(t0))/h. The limit is exactly the derivative criterion for E at the interior time t0, so E'(t0)=∫e_t(x,t0) dx. This is the derivative of the actual finite energy, not just a formal integral expression. Arbitrary t0 proves N003.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M001\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"F\",\"b\",\"e_t\",\"E\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n×ℝ,W=ℝ,S=Q×I,f=e_t on positive times and zero elsewhere.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean product space is topological and ℝ is seminormed.\",\"Q×I is compact, as a finite product of closed bounded boxes.\",\"N002 gives smoothness of e_t on an open neighborhood of Q×I, all of whose times are positive.\"]},{\"input\":\"M002\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"V=K=W=ℝ,S=I,c=B,f(r)=e(x,r) for fixed x∈Q, extended by zero outside positive times; p=t0,r=t0+h; L(r) is multiplication by e_t(x,r).\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Real scalar spaces satisfy all source normed and real-like structure premises.\",\"N002 supplies the classical time derivative e_t on I; its full derivative restricts to a derivative within I.\",\"F001 with the chosen B bounds |e_t(x,r)| on Q×I; the scalar multiplication map has that same operator norm.\",\"I is a real closed interval, hence convex.\",\"t0 belongs to I by η>0.\",\"0<|h|<η puts t0+h in I.\"]},{\"input\":\"M001\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℝ^n,μ=Lebesgue volume,G=ℝ,I(source index)=ℝ,l=punctured neighborhoods of 0,F=the local F,f(x)=e_t(x,t0),bound=b.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean Lebesgue space is measurable and ℝ is a real normed space.\",\"The punctured intervals of radii 1/k give the countable basis displayed in S003.\",\"F002 gives continuity of F(h) for all sufficiently small nonzero h, hence a.e. strong measurability.\",\"F002 proves the bound everywhere for all sufficiently small nonzero h.\",\"F002 proves integrability of b.\",\"F002 proves convergence for every x, stronger than almost everywhere.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R004\",\"inputs\":[\"N002\",\"M003\",\"M006\",\"M009\",\"M010\"],\"output\":\"N004\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary j∈{1,…,n}.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose C from N002 for t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose its enclosing radius R>0 from N002.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"C\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed spatial box\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"g\",\"type\":\"smooth map ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set g(x)=u_t(x,t0)·u_j(j,x,t0).\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"u_t\",\"u_j\",\"j\",\"t0\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"d\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set d(x)=∂_{x_j}g(x).\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"g\",\"j\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"ι\",\"type\":\"coordinate insertion bijection ℝ×ℝ^(n−1)→ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Let ι(z,y) put z in coordinate j and the coordinates of y in the remaining positions in increasing order.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"j\"]},{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^(n−1)\",\"scope\":\"R004/coordinate section\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary remaining coordinates y to evaluate the one-variable section.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0 and j∈{1,…,n}; N002 gives n≥1 and the compact set C, radius R>0, and Q=[−R,R]^n. Define g(x)=u_t(x,t0)·u_j(j,x,t0) and d=∂_{x_j}g. They are smooth by N002. Apply M006 along the jth spatial coordinate line to the sections of u_t and u_j. The product rule gives d=v_j·u_j+u_t·u_jj. N002 states integrability of d and of its two summands, and also g=0 outside C. Since C⊆int Q, g vanishes on an open neighborhood of the boundary of Q, in particular at each pair of opposite j-coordinate faces.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"j\",\"t0\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"g\",\"d\",\"u_t\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"d(x)=v_j(j,x,t0)·u_j(j,x,t0)+u_t(x,t0)·u_jj(j,x,t0). The three scalar functions in this equality are continuous, integrable, and zero outside C. The function g is smooth and zero off C⊆int Q, so it vanishes at the j-coordinate endpoints ±R for every choice of the remaining coordinates.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"d\",\"v_j\",\"u_j\",\"u_t\",\"u_jj\",\"j\",\"x\",\"t0\",\"C\",\"Q\",\"g\",\"R\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Define the insertion map ι. Fix arbitrary remaining coordinates y∈ℝ^(n−1). The function z↦g(ι(z,y)) is smooth on ℝ and its derivative is z↦d(ι(z,y)). The derivative is continuous on [−R,R], hence interval-integrable there. Apply M003 with endpoints −R,R: ∫_{−R}^R d(ι(z,y)) dz=g(ι(R,y))−g(ι(−R,y))=0 by F001. These sections of d vanish when |z|>R. Thus their full real integrals equal the interval integrals and are zero. The oriented interval integral here equals the Lebesgue integral on [−R,R], since −R<R and the two endpoints have measure zero. This argument holds for every y; when n=1 there are no remaining coordinates and ℝ^0 is a one-point product with total measure one.\",\"uses\":[\"M003\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"j\",\"g\",\"d\",\"ι\",\"y\",\"R\",\"Q\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"For every y0∈ℝ^(n−1), the section z↦d(ι(z,y0)) is integrable on ℝ and ∫_ℝ d(ι(z,y0)) dz=0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"y0\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^(n−1)\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"y0\",\"z\",\"d\",\"ι\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Under the coordinate extraction inverse to ι, M009 identifies Lebesgue measure on ℝ^n with Lebesgue measure on ℝ times the product measure on ℝ^(n−1). Use k=n−1, each coordinate space ℝ, and index j−1 in Fin n; n≥1 makes n=(n−1)+1. Hence the integrable function d from F001 has the same integral and integrability as (z,y)↦d(ι(z,y)) on the product. Apply M010 to that product function; its integral is ∫_{ℝ^(n−1)}(∫_ℝ d(ι(z,y)) dz)dy=0 by F002. Equivalently this is the supplied Fubini calculation over Q, since every term is zero outside Q and all terms are absolutely integrable. In particular ∫d=0. By F001 and linearity for integrable functions, 0=∫v_j·u_j+∫u_t·u_jj. Symmetry of the real dot product and rearrangement give ∫u_j·v_j=−∫u_t·u_jj, with all integrals finite. Arbitrary t0 and j yield N004.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M009\",\"M010\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"j\",\"t0\",\"d\",\"g\",\"ι\",\"Q\",\"u_j\",\"v_j\",\"u_t\",\"u_jj\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ,W=ℝ^m; f and g(source) are the j-coordinate sections of u_t(·,t0) and u_j(j,·,t0).\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean ℝ^m is a real inner-product and normed space.\",\"N002 exports smoothness of u_t, giving differentiability of its spatial section.\",\"N002 exports smoothness of u_j, giving differentiability of its spatial section.\"]},{\"input\":\"M003\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"W=ℝ,f(z)=g(ι(z,y)),a=−R,b=R.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℝ is a complete real normed space.\",\"F001 and the coordinate insertion give a smooth section on all ℝ, so differentiability holds including endpoints.\",\"Its derivative d∘ι is continuous on [−R,R] by F001, hence integrable on this compact finite-measure interval.\"]},{\"input\":\"M009\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"k=n−1,A_i=ℝ for every i∈Fin n,i=j−1; the native extraction map is ι inverse.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Every coordinate has the standard real Lebesgue measure-space structure.\",\"Real Lebesgue measure is sigma-finite, by the increasing cover by bounded intervals.\",\"N002 gives n≥1 and j∈{1,…,n}, so the converted index is in Fin((n−1)+1).\"]},{\"input\":\"M010\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℝ,B=ℝ^(n−1),μ and ν their Lebesgue measures,W=ℝ,f(z,y)=d(ι(z,y)).\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"These are standard measurable product spaces with sigma-finite, hence s-finite, Lebesgue measures; the empty product for n=1 has measure one; ℝ is a real normed space.\",\"F001 gives integrability of d on ℝ^n, and M009 in S003 transports it through the volume-preserving measurable coordinate equivalence.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R005\",\"inputs\":[\"N001\",\"N002\",\"N003\",\"N004\",\"M002\"],\"output\":\"P\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R005/pointwise energy derivative\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S001\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary t0>0 for the derivative computation.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"s\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R005/constancy\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary positive s.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R005/constancy\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary positive t.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R005/constancy\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define f(r)=E(r) for r>0 and f(r)=0 for r≤0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"E\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0. N002 already proves integrability of e at every positive time and supplies the definition guard for E, as well as integrability of every term in the following calculation. N003 supplies the derivative of the finite energy. Substitute the density formula from N002 and then N004 for each spatial index. By linearity and finite summation,\\nE'(t0)=∫e_t(x,t0)dx\\n=2∫u_t·u_tt dx+2∑_{j=1}^n∫u_j·v_j dx\\n=2∫u_t·u_tt dx−2∑_{j=1}^n∫u_t·u_jj dx\\n=2∫u_t·(u_tt−Δu) dx=0.\\nThe penultimate equality uses the definition Δu=∑_j u_jj and bilinearity of the dot product. The last integrand is zero at every point by N001, so its integral is zero. This retains the factor two and the energy normalization without inserting a half. Since t0 was arbitrary, E is differentiable with zero derivative at every positive time.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N002\",\"N003\",\"N004\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"n\",\"E\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"Δu\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"For every r>0, E is differentiable at r and E'(r)=0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"E\",\"r\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"For completeness retain the supplied constancy consequence. Fix arbitrary s,t>0 and define the zero extension f of E as declared. On the closed interval with endpoints s,t, all times are positive. F001 says f is differentiable there with derivative zero; locality justifies use of the extension. Apply M002 with V=K=W=ℝ, this convex interval, c=0, and L identically zero. It gives |E(t)−E(s)|≤0·|t−s|=0, so E(t)=E(s), including the case s=t. Thus E is constant on (0,∞). Together with N002's integrability and F001 this proves P; no endpoint t=0 was used or asserted.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"s\",\"t\",\"f\",\"E\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M002\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"V=K=W=ℝ,f the zero extension of E,S the unordered closed interval between s,t,c=0,L(r)=0,p=s,r(source)=t.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The scalar real spaces satisfy all normed-space and real-like field conditions.\",\"F001 gives derivative zero at every point of S, since min(s,t)>0; restrict this full derivative to S.\",\"The zero operator has norm zero, so the bound is 0≤0.\",\"A real closed interval is convex, including a singleton.\",\"s is an endpoint of S.\",\"t is an endpoint of S.\"]}]}]}}}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":15153,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0b9ca2ffc961a3af016ae49f7f1fd48191abd6b99ba8d47cb1","call_id":"call_475f51ea4b5445a8ae89e9b806a7630c","name":"check_draft","arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"spec\":{\"format_version\":2,\"case_id\":\"wave-map-energy\",\"full_target\":\"A sphere-valued map cannot vanish outside a compact set. As approved by\\nthe user on 2026-10-23, interpret spatial compact support as compact support\\nof a perturbation from a **fixed unit vector**, locally uniformly in time.\\nThis is a disclosed nontrivial interpretation, not a claimed author-issued\\ncorrection.\\n\\nLet \\\\(n,m\\\\geq1\\\\) be integers and let\\n\\\\(u:\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\times(0,\\\\infty)\\\\to\\\\mathbb R^m\\\\) be jointly smooth.\\nFix \\\\(q\\\\in\\\\mathbb R^m\\\\) with \\\\(|q|=1\\\\). Assume:\\n\\n1. \\\\(|u(x,t)|=1\\\\) for all \\\\(x\\\\) and \\\\(t>0\\\\).\\n2. The acceleration \\\\(u_{tt}-\\\\Delta_xu\\\\) is normal to the sphere at\\n   \\\\(u\\\\): at each point there is a real scalar \\\\(a(x,t)\\\\) with\\n   \\\\(u_{tt}-\\\\Delta_xu=a u\\\\). No regularity of the selected scalar is\\n   assumed.\\n3. For every \\\\(0<s<t<\\\\infty\\\\), a compact set\\n   \\\\(C_{[s,t]}\\\\subset\\\\mathbb R^n\\\\) exists such that \\\\(u(x,r)=q\\\\) for\\n   \\\\(x\\\\notin C_{[s,t]}\\\\), \\\\(r\\\\in[s,t]\\\\).\\n\\nWith Euclidean norms and Lebesgue measure, define\\n\\n\\\\[\\ne(x,t)=|u_t(x,t)|^2+\\\\sum_{j=1}^n|\\\\partial_{x_j}u(x,t)|^2,\\n\\\\qquad E(t)=\\\\int_{\\\\mathbb R^n}e(x,t)\\\\,dx.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe spatial gradient square is the Frobenius square, and\\n\\\\(\\\\Delta_xu=\\\\sum_j\\\\partial_{x_jx_j}u\\\\) component by component.\\nProve integrability of \\\\(e(\\\\cdot,t)\\\\), differentiability of \\\\(E\\\\),\\nand \\\\(E'(t)=0\\\\) for every \\\\(t>0\\\\). No assertion at time zero is required.\",\"conclusion\":\"For every real t>0, e(·,t) is Lebesgue integrable on ℝ^n, E is differentiable at t, and E'(t)=0. In particular E is constant on (0,∞). No assertion at time zero is made.\",\"parameters\":[{\"symbol\":\"n\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"u\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m; the Euclidean spaces carry their standard dot products, norms, topology, and Lebesgue measures\"},{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"vector in ℝ^m\"}],\"hypotheses\":[{\"id\":\"H001\",\"statement\":\"n≥1 and m≥1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:25\"},{\"id\":\"H002\",\"statement\":\"u is jointly smooth on the open set ℝ^n × (0,∞).\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:25-26\"},{\"id\":\"H003\",\"statement\":\"|q|=1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:27\"},{\"id\":\"H004\",\"statement\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and every t>0, |u(x,t)|=1.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:29\"},{\"id\":\"H005\",\"statement\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and every t>0 there exists a real scalar a such that u_tt(x,t)−Δu(x,t)=a u(x,t). No regularity of such scalars as a function of (x,t) is assumed.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:30-33\"},{\"id\":\"H006\",\"statement\":\"For every pair of real numbers 0<s<t<∞ there exists a compact set C⊆ℝ^n such that u(x,r)=q whenever x∉C and r∈[s,t].\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:34-36\"}],\"definitions\":[{\"id\":\"D001\",\"symbol\":\"u_t\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"u_t(x,t)=∂u(x,t)/∂t, component by component. Derivatives are the usual real derivatives; a derivative not existing is assigned the total derivative convention value zero. Under H002 these are classical derivatives.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\",\"m\",\"x\",\"t\"]},{\"id\":\"D002\",\"symbol\":\"u_tt\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"u_tt(x,t)=∂u_t(x,t)/∂t, component by component, with the same total derivative convention.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D001\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_t\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D003\",\"symbol\":\"u_j\",\"type\":\"family indexed by j∈{1,…,n} of maps ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"u_j(j,x,t)=∂_{x_j}u(x,t), the derivative along the jth standard coordinate direction, component by component with the total derivative convention. We also write u_j when the index j is explicitly fixed.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\",\"m\",\"j\",\"x\",\"t\"]},{\"id\":\"D004\",\"symbol\":\"u_jj\",\"type\":\"family indexed by j∈{1,…,n} of maps ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"u_jj(j,x,t)=∂_{x_j}u_j(j,x,t), with the total derivative convention. The notation u_jj with fixed j has this same meaning.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D003\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_j\",\"j\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D005\",\"symbol\":\"v_j\",\"type\":\"family indexed by j∈{1,…,n} of maps ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"v_j(j,x,t)=∂_{x_j}u_t(x,t), with the total derivative convention. With fixed j we abbreviate this by v_j.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D001\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_t\",\"j\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D006\",\"symbol\":\"Δu\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ^m\",\"definition\":\"Δu(x,t)=∑_{j=1}^n u_jj(j,x,t), the componentwise spatial Laplacian Δ_xu of the input.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D004\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_jj\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D007\",\"symbol\":\"e\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ\",\"definition\":\"e(x,t)=|u_t(x,t)|²+∑_{j=1}^n |u_j(j,x,t)|². Here |z|²=z·z is the Euclidean norm square on ℝ^m, so the spatial sum is exactly the Frobenius square |Du|². All integrals in the proof are Lebesgue integrals, and dx denotes standard volume on ℝ^n.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D001\",\"D003\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_t\",\"u_j\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"D008\",\"symbol\":\"e_t\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n × (0,∞) → ℝ\",\"definition\":\"e_t(x,t)=∂e(x,t)/∂t, using the total derivative convention, whose classical existence and formula will be proved.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D007\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"x\",\"t\",\"n\"]},{\"id\":\"D009\",\"symbol\":\"E\",\"type\":\"map (0,∞) → ℝ\",\"definition\":\"For t>0, E(t)=∫_{ℝ^n}e(x,t) dx. This is the finite Lebesgue integral, used only after N002 has proved integrability at every positive time; no definition at time zero is required.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D007\"],\"requires\":[\"N002\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number in (0,∞)\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"n\",\"t\"]}],\"logic\":{\"classical\":true,\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\",\"implication_introduction\"]},\"pins\":{},\"inputs\":[]},\"graph\":{\"format_version\":2,\"case_id\":\"wave-map-energy\",\"spec_sha256\":\"controller-bound\",\"propositions\":[{\"id\":\"H001\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\"]},{\"id\":\"H002\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\"]},{\"id\":\"H003\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"q\"]},{\"id\":\"H004\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"n\",\"x\",\"t\"]},{\"id\":\"H005\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[\"D002\",\"D006\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar, existentially quantified at each (x,t)\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"u_tt\",\"Δu\",\"x\",\"t\",\"a\",\"n\"]},{\"id\":\"H006\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"s\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"subset of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"q\",\"n\",\"s\",\"t\",\"C\",\"x\",\"r\"]},{\"id\":\"M001\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A be a measurable space with measure μ and G a normed additive commutative group and real normed space. Let I be a type, l a countably generated filter on I, F:I→A→G, f:A→G and b:A→ℝ. If eventually along l each F(i) is μ-almost-everywhere strongly measurable, eventually along l |F(i)(x)|≤b(x) for μ-almost every x, b is μ-integrable, and for μ-almost every x the values F(i)(x) tend along l to f(x), then ∫F(i)dμ tends along l to ∫f dμ.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"measure on A\"},{\"symbol\":\"G\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group with real normed-space structure\"},{\"symbol\":\"I\",\"type\":\"type\"},{\"symbol\":\"l\",\"type\":\"filter on I\"},{\"symbol\":\"F\",\"type\":\"map I→A→G\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map A→G\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"map A→ℝ\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"element of A\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"μ\",\"G\",\"I\",\"l\",\"F\",\"f\",\"b\",\"i\",\"x\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.tendsto_integral_filter_of_dominated_convergence\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A has a measurable-space structure and μ is a measure; G is a normed additive commutative group with a real normed-space structure.\",\"l is countably generated.\",\"F(i) is μ-a.e. strongly measurable eventually along l.\",\"The norm bound |F(i)(x)|≤b(x) holds μ-a.e. eventually along l.\",\"b is μ-integrable.\",\"F(i)(x) tends along l to f(x) for μ-almost every x.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M002\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let V be a real normed additive commutative group with a normed-space structure over a nontrivially normed field K that is real-like or complex-like, and let W be a normed additive commutative group and normed K-space. For f:V→W, a set S⊆V, a real c, points p,r∈V and L:V→(V→L[K]W), assume f has Fréchet derivative L(z) within S at every z∈S, |L(z)|≤c for every z∈S, S is real convex, and p,r∈S. Then |f(r)−f(p)|≤c|r−p|.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"real normed space also a normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"nontrivially normed field with IsRCLikeNormedField structure\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map V→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"S\",\"type\":\"subset of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"p\",\"type\":\"point of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"point of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"L\",\"type\":\"map V→ continuous K-linear maps V→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"point of V\"}],\"objects\":[\"V\",\"K\",\"W\",\"f\",\"S\",\"c\",\"p\",\"r\",\"L\",\"z\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_hasFDerivWithin_le\",\"hypotheses\":[\"V is a real normed space and a normed K-space; K is a nontrivially normed field with IsRCLikeNormedField; W is a normed K-space.\",\"f has Fréchet derivative L(z) within S at every z∈S.\",\"For every z∈S, the operator norm |L(z)|≤c.\",\"S is convex over ℝ.\",\"p belongs to S.\",\"r belongs to S.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M003\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let W be a complete real normed space and f:ℝ→W, with real endpoints a,b. If f is differentiable at every point of the unordered closed interval with endpoints a,b and deriv f is interval-integrable with respect to real Lebesgue measure from a to b, then the oriented interval integral ∫_a^b deriv f(z) dz equals f(b)−f(a).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"complete real normed space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"W\",\"f\",\"a\",\"b\",\"z\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"intervalIntegral.integral_deriv_eq_sub\",\"hypotheses\":[\"W is a normed additive commutative group, real normed space and complete space.\",\"f is differentiable at every point of the unordered closed interval between a and b.\",\"deriv f is Lebesgue interval-integrable from a to b.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M005\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let X be a topological space with a measurable-space structure for which open sets are measurable, let μ be a measure finite on compact sets, and let W be a normed additive commutative group. Every continuous f:X→W having compact support (the closure of {x:f(x)≠0} is compact) is μ-integrable.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"X\",\"type\":\"topological and measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"measure on X\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map X→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of X\"}],\"objects\":[\"X\",\"μ\",\"W\",\"f\",\"x\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Continuous.integrable_of_hasCompactSupport\",\"hypotheses\":[\"X is topological and measurable with open sets measurable; μ is finite on compact sets; W is a normed additive commutative group.\",\"f is continuous.\",\"The closure of the nonzero set of f is compact.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M006\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let K be an RCLike field, W an inner product space over K (and normed additive commutative group) also carrying a real normed-space structure, and f,g:ℝ→W. If f and g are real differentiable at a real point z, then deriv(t↦⟨f(t),g(t)⟩_K)(z)=⟨f(z),deriv g(z)⟩_K+⟨deriv f(z),g(z)⟩_K.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"RCLike field\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group, inner product space over K, and real normed space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"g\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"W\",\"f\",\"g\",\"z\",\"t\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"deriv_inner_apply\",\"hypotheses\":[\"K is RCLike; W has the stated normed group, K-inner-product and real normed-space structures.\",\"f is real differentiable at z.\",\"g is real differentiable at z.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M007\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let K be a nontrivially normed field and V,W normed additive commutative groups and normed K-spaces. Let f:V→W, z∈V, and k∈ℕ∞ω. If f is ContDiffAt K k at z and minSmoothness K 2≤k, then its second Fréchet derivative at z is symmetric: for every v,w∈V, D(Df)(z)(v)(w)=D(Df)(z)(w)(v). Here ContDiffAt is the native local smoothness predicate and minSmoothness K 2 is 2 for real-like/complex-like fields and ω otherwise.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"nontrivially normed field\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and normed K-space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map V→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"point of V\"},{\"symbol\":\"k\",\"type\":\"smoothness index in ℕ∞ω\"},{\"symbol\":\"v\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"},{\"symbol\":\"w\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"}],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"V\",\"W\",\"f\",\"z\",\"k\",\"v\",\"w\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"ContDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt\",\"hypotheses\":[\"K is a nontrivially normed field; V and W are normed additive commutative groups and normed K-spaces.\",\"f is ContDiffAt K k at z.\",\"minSmoothness K 2≤k.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M008\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let X be a topological space, W a seminormed additive group, S⊆X compact, and f:X→W continuous on S. There exists a real c such that |f(x)|≤c for every x∈S.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"X\",\"type\":\"topological space\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"seminormed additive group\"},{\"symbol\":\"S\",\"type\":\"subset of X\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map X→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of X\"}],\"objects\":[\"X\",\"W\",\"S\",\"f\",\"c\",\"x\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"IsCompact.exists_bound_of_continuousOn\",\"hypotheses\":[\"X is a topological space and W is a seminormed additive group.\",\"S is compact.\",\"f is continuous on S.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M009\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For every natural k, family A_i of measure spaces indexed by i∈Fin(k+1), each with sigma-finite volume, and index i∈Fin(k+1), the measurable equivalence which sends a tuple x to (x_i,(x_{i.succAbove(j)})_{j∈Fin k}) preserves volume from the finite product of the A_i to A_i times the product of the remaining spaces. Here i.succAbove lists the remaining indices in order.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"k\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"family of measure spaces indexed by Fin(k+1)\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of Fin(k+1)\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"tuple in the product of A\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"element of Fin k\"}],\"objects\":[\"k\",\"A\",\"i\",\"x\",\"j\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.volume_preserving_piFinSuccAbove\",\"hypotheses\":[\"Each A_i is a measure space.\",\"Each coordinate volume is sigma-finite.\",\"i is an index in Fin(k+1).\"]}},{\"id\":\"M010\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A,B be measurable spaces with s-finite measures μ,ν respectively, and W a normed additive commutative group and real normed space. For every f:A×B→W integrable with respect to μ×ν, ∫_{A×B}f d(μ×ν)=∫_B(∫_A f(x,y)dμ(x))dν(y).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"measurable space\"},{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"s-finite measure on A\"},{\"symbol\":\"ν\",\"type\":\"s-finite measure on B\"},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"normed additive commutative group and real normed space\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map A×B→W\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"element of A\"},{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"element of B\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"B\",\"μ\",\"ν\",\"W\",\"f\",\"x\",\"y\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.integral_prod_symm\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A,B are measurable spaces with s-finite measures μ,ν; W is a normed additive commutative group and real normed space.\",\"f is integrable for the product measure μ×ν.\"]}},{\"id\":\"N001\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every x∈ℝ^n and t>0, u(x,t)·u_t(x,t)=0 and u_t(x,t)·(u_tt(x,t)−Δu(x,t))=0. Moreover u·u_tt=−|u_t|² and u·u_jj(j,x,t)=−|u_j(j,x,t)|² for every j∈{1,…,n}. Every scalar a satisfying u_tt−Δu=a u at (x,t) necessarily equals ∑_{j=1}^n|u_j(j,x,t)|²−|u_t(x,t)|²; thus the explicit sphere wave-map equation is a consequence, not an extra assumption.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D006\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"Δu\",\"x\",\"t\",\"j\",\"a\",\"n\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:52-68; tangency and the optional explicit PDE calculation are retained.\"},{\"id\":\"N002\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"n≥1 and m≥1. The map u and all its partial derivatives are smooth on ℝ^n×(0,∞); in particular ∂_t u_j(j,x,t)=v_j(j,x,t) and e_t(x,t)=2u_t(x,t)·u_tt(x,t)+2∑_{j=1}^n u_j(j,x,t)·v_j(j,x,t). For every t0>0, let J=[t0/2,3t0/2]. There exist a compact C⊆ℝ^n and R>0 such that C⊆(−R,R)^n, u(x,r)=q for x∉C and r∈J, and every positive-order derivative of u vanishes for x∉C and r∈int J. Put Q=[−R,R]^n, whose Lebesgue measure is (2R)^n. For every r∈int J the following scalar spatial functions are continuous, zero outside C, and Lebesgue integrable: e(·,r), e_t(·,r), u_t(·,r)·u_tt(·,r), and, for each j, u_j(j,·,r)·v_j(j,·,r), u_t(·,r)·u_jj(j,·,r), u_t(·,r)·u_j(j,·,r), and ∂_{x_j}(u_t(·,r)·u_j(j,·,r)). Their absolute integrals are finite, each bounded by a finite supremum on Q times (2R)^n. In particular e(·,t) is integrable for every t>0.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"J\",\"type\":\"closed time interval\"},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real number, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed spatial box\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real time\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"u\",\"q\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"Δu\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"x\",\"t\",\"t0\",\"J\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"r\",\"j\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:70-94; common support, integrability, and the differentiated density.\"},{\"id\":\"N003\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every t0>0 the finite integral E is differentiable at t0 and E'(t0)=∫_{ℝ^n}e_t(x,t0) dx.\",\"definitions\":[\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"E\",\"e_t\",\"t0\",\"x\",\"n\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:87-117; bounded difference quotients and dominated convergence.\"},{\"id\":\"N004\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"For every t0>0 and j∈{1,…,n}, both integrands below are Lebesgue integrable and ∫_{ℝ^n}u_j(j,x,t0)·v_j(j,x,t0) dx=−∫_{ℝ^n}u_t(x,t0)·u_jj(j,x,t0) dx.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"u_j\",\"v_j\",\"u_t\",\"u_jj\",\"t0\",\"j\",\"x\",\"n\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:119-142; coordinatewise FTC and Fubini integration by parts.\"},{\"id\":\"P\",\"kind\":\"target\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"E\",\"n\",\"t\"]}],\"inferences\":[{\"id\":\"R001\",\"inputs\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"H005\",\"M006\"],\"output\":\"N001\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D006\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R001/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix an arbitrary spatial point x.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R001/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix an arbitrary t>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"real scalar\",\"scope\":\"R001/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose a scalar a given by H005 at (x,t), so u_tt(x,t)−Δu(x,t)=a u(x,t).\",\"from_inputs\":[\"H005\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"u\",\"u_tt\",\"Δu\"]},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\",\"scope\":\"R001/S003 spatial calculation\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"For the spatial second-derivative identity, fix any j∈{1,…,n}.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix arbitrary x∈ℝ^n and t>0. H004 gives u·u=1 on the whole domain. The time section is differentiable near t by H002. Applying M006 with both functions equal to this section gives 0=∂_t(u·u)=2u·u_t, hence u·u_t=0. Whenever a source is formulated for a function on all of ℝ, a time section may be set to zero for nonpositive times; it agrees with the given section on a neighborhood of t, so its derivative at t is unchanged. This convention is only for applying sources and does not define u at time zero.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"For every spatial point y∈ℝ^n and every positive time r, u(y,r)·u_t(y,r)=0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"u_t\",\"y\",\"r\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose a from H005 at (x,t). Dot its identity with u_t. F001 at (x,t) and symmetry of the Euclidean dot product give u_t·(u_tt−Δu)=a(u_t·u)=0. Only this pointwise choice is used; a is never differentiated.\",\"uses\":[\"H005\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"a\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"Δu\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"u_t(x,t)·(u_tt(x,t)−Δu(x,t))=0.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"Δu\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Retain also the supplied explicit equation calculation. Since F001 is an identity on an open time interval, M006 applied to the time sections of u and u_t gives 0=∂_t(u·u_t)=|u_t|²+u·u_tt, so u·u_tt=−|u_t|². For any fixed j, apply M006 first to u with itself along a spatial coordinate line, obtaining u·u_j=0 from H004, and then to u and u_j along that line, obtaining u·u_jj=−|u_j|². All sections and their derivatives are differentiable by H002. Dot the equation defining a with u. Since u·u=1, a=u·u_tt−∑_j u·u_jj=∑_j|u_j|²−|u_t|². The last computation works for every scalar satisfying the normality equation, and does not give it any assumed regularity. Together with F001 and F002, universal introduction in x,t and the arbitrary spatial index proves N001.\",\"uses\":[\"H002\",\"H004\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"t\",\"j\",\"a\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"Δu\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"id\":\"A001\",\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, W=ℝ^m; f=g is the time section r↦u(x,r), extended by zero for r≤0; z=t.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The real field and Euclidean ℝ^m have the required real inner-product and normed structures.\",\"H002 on the open positive-time domain gives differentiability of f at t>0.\",\"g=f, so the same differentiability holds.\"]},{\"id\":\"A002\",\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ, W=ℝ^m. First use f(r)=u(x,r), g(r)=u_t(x,r), z=t; also use along each spatial line first f=g=u and then f=u,g=u_j, at the coordinate of x.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"All these sections take values in Euclidean ℝ^m, a real inner-product and normed space.\",\"H002 gives differentiability of each section of u at the displayed point.\",\"H002 gives differentiability of u_t or u_j, and also of u when g=u.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R002\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"H006\",\"M005\",\"M006\",\"M007\",\"M008\"],\"output\":\"N002\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix any t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"J\",\"type\":\"closed interval in ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set J=[t0/2,3t0/2].\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose C with the common support property in F001.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"J\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose R>0 with C⊆(−R,R)^n from F002.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed box in ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R002/argument\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S004\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Put Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"n\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"H001 supplies n,m≥1. Fix t0>0 and J=[t0/2,3t0/2]. The endpoints satisfy 0<t0/2<3t0/2, so H006 gives a compact spatial set common to all times in J. Separately, H002 ensures that all partial derivatives of u are smooth on the open domain. M007 over ℝ at each point, applied to the spatial and time coordinate vectors, gives ∂_t u_j=v_j. The input is locally smooth at that point, so it is in particular C² there; if a total ambient map is needed, extend u by zero off the positive-time domain, which leaves it smooth on a neighborhood of the point. Differentiating each squared norm using M006, and the finite-sum differentiation rule, therefore gives e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2∑_j u_j·v_j throughout the domain. These formulas also show e and e_t are smooth.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"H006\",\"M006\",\"M007\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"t0\",\"J\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"v_j\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"q\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"There exists a compact C0⊆ℝ^n such that u(y,r)=q for every y∉C0 and r∈J.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"C0\",\"type\":\"subset of ℝ^n, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"real time in J\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"C0\",\"y\",\"r\",\"J\",\"u\",\"q\"],\"assumptions\":[]},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"statement\":\"All partial derivatives of u are smooth on the open positive-time domain; ∂_t u_j=v_j and e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2∑_{j=1}^n u_j·v_j at every point. The functions e and e_t are smooth there.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"u\",\"u_j\",\"v_j\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose C from F001. Apply M008 on C to the identity map into Euclidean ℝ^n. It gives a finite real bound on |x| for x∈C. Increase this bound, if necessary, to a positive R larger than it. Since |x_j|≤|x|, C⊆(−R,R)^n. Thus such a positive R exists, even if C is empty.\",\"uses\":[\"M008\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"There exists R0>0 such that C⊆(−R0,R0)^n.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"R0\",\"type\":\"real number, existential\"}],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"n\",\"R0\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Choose R from F002 and set Q=[−R,R]^n. This closed box is compact, measurable, and has finite Lebesgue measure (2R)^n. The compact set C is closed in ℝ^n. On the open set (ℝ^n\\\\C)×int J, F001 says u=q. At every point of this open set u is constant in a neighborhood; differentiating the constant on that neighborhood, successively in each coordinate, gives zero for every positive-order derivative. Consequently at any r∈int J all the functions listed in N002 vanish outside C: every summand contains such a derivative. For the last function, the product rule expands it into v_j·u_j+u_t·u_jj, both of which vanish there. Continuity and smoothness follow from F003 and smoothness of the dot product.\",\"uses\":[],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\",\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"J\",\"n\",\"u\",\"q\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"e\",\"e_t\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F004\",\"statement\":\"C⊆int Q, Q is compact and measurable with finite Lebesgue measure (2R)^n, and for every r∈int J all the scalar spatial functions listed in N002 are continuous and vanish outside C. In particular their nonzero sets have closures contained in the compact set C.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"time in int J\"}],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"Q\",\"R\",\"n\",\"J\",\"r\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"e\",\"e_t\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S004\",\"text\":\"For every r∈int J apply M005 to each continuous scalar function in F004, on ℝ^n with Lebesgue measure. Its support closure is a closed subset of compact C, hence compact; Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets. Each is therefore integrable. Equivalently its norm is bounded on Q by M008 and it vanishes outside Q, so its absolute integral is at most that finite bound times |Q|=(2R)^n. This also supplies the stated finiteness estimate for all terms. Since t0 belongs to int J, e(·,t0) is integrable. As t0 was arbitrary, this holds at every positive time. Together with F003 and F004 this proves the complete statement N002, including the common support witnesses and all derivative-product integrability assertions.\",\"uses\":[\"M005\",\"M008\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F003\",\"F004\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"m\",\"u\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"C\",\"Q\",\"R\",\"J\",\"t0\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ,W=ℝ^m; for each spatial point take f=g equal to the time section of u_t or of u_j, respectively.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean ℝ^m has the required inner-product and real normed-space structures.\",\"Each section f is differentiable by H002 at every positive time.\",\"g=f has the same differentiability.\"]},{\"input\":\"M007\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ,V=ℝ^n×ℝ,W=ℝ^m,f the zero extension of u, z any point of the positive-time domain,k=2; evaluate symmetry on the jth spatial coordinate and the time coordinate.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The real field and finite-dimensional Euclidean domain and codomain have the required normed-space structures.\",\"H002 gives local C² regularity at z since the time coordinate of z is positive; the extension agrees with u on an open neighborhood of z.\",\"minSmoothness ℝ 2=2≤2.\"]},{\"id\":\"A001\",\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n,W=ℝ^n,S=C,f the identity.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean space is topological and normed.\",\"C is compact by the witness chosen from F001.\",\"The identity is continuous on C.\"]},{\"input\":\"M005\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n,μ=Lebesgue volume,W=ℝ,f successively each scalar section listed in F004, at each r∈int J.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The Borel/Lebesgue measurable structure makes open sets measurable; Euclidean Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets; ℝ is a normed additive commutative group.\",\"F004 proves continuity of every section.\",\"F004 places the closure of each nonzero set in compact C; it is closed and hence compact.\"]},{\"id\":\"A002\",\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n,W=ℝ,S=Q,f each scalar section in F004.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The domain is Euclidean and the codomain ℝ is seminormed.\",\"F004 proves Q compact.\",\"F004 proves each section continuous, in particular on Q.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R003\",\"inputs\":[\"N002\",\"M001\",\"M002\",\"M008\"],\"output\":\"N003\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose C from N002 at t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose the corresponding R from N002 so C⊆(−R,R)^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"C\",\"n\",\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed spatial box\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"η\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set η=t0/4.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\"]},{\"symbol\":\"I\",\"type\":\"closed time interval\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set I=[t0−η,t0+η].\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"η\"]},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"nonnegative real number\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose B≥0 satisfying F001.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"e_t\",\"Q\",\"I\"]},{\"symbol\":\"F\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"For 0<|h|<η define F(h)(x)=(e(x,t0+h)−e(x,t0))/h; for other h define F(h)(x)=0. This is total, since every t0+h in the first case is positive.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R003/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define b(x)=B 1_Q(x), where 1_Q equals one on Q and zero off Q.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"B\",\"Q\",\"n\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0 and choose C,R from N002. Put Q=[−R,R]^n, η=t0/4 and I=[t0−η,t0+η]. The elementary endpoint inequalities place I strictly inside (t0/2,3t0/2). N002 makes E a finite integral at every positive time, so its guarded definition is now available. Its density and e_t are continuous on the compact set Q×I. M008 applied there to e_t gives a finite bound; replacing it by its maximum with zero gives a B≥0 with |e_t(x,r)|≤B on Q×I.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M008\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"η\",\"I\",\"n\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"E\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"There exists B0≥0 such that |e_t(x,r)|≤B0 for every x∈Q and r∈I.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"B0\",\"type\":\"real number, existential\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of Q\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"time in I\"}],\"objects\":[\"B0\",\"e_t\",\"x\",\"r\",\"Q\",\"I\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose B as in F001 and define F and b as in the local declarations. For any x∈Q, the scalar time section r↦e(x,r) is differentiable on I by N002, with derivative e_t(x,r). Apply M002 on the convex interval I, with derivative the linear map multiplying by e_t(x,r), whose operator norm is |e_t(x,r)|≤B. For 0<|h|<η both t0 and t0+h lie in I, so |e(x,t0+h)−e(x,t0)|≤B|h|. Division by |h| gives |F(h)(x)|≤B. Outside Q, N002 gives both densities zero, since both times lie inside the common time slab. Hence |F(h)(x)|≤b(x) on all of ℝ^n. The function b is integrable: Q is measurable of finite measure (2R)^n and ∫|b|=B(2R)^n<∞. For each such h, F(h) is continuous and integrable, by N002's integrability at its two times and linearity. At each x, the definition of e_t as the classical derivative gives F(h)(x)→e_t(x,t0) as h→0 through nonzero values.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"B\",\"F\",\"b\",\"n\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"I\",\"Q\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"b is Lebesgue integrable; for all real h with 0<|h|<η, F(h) is continuous and integrable and |F(h)(x)|≤b(x) for every x∈ℝ^n. For every x∈ℝ^n, F(h)(x) tends to e_t(x,t0) as h→0 through h≠0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"h\",\"type\":\"real number\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"b\",\"F\",\"h\",\"x\",\"η\",\"e_t\",\"t0\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Use M001 with parameter filter the punctured neighborhood filter h→0, functions F(h), limit e_t(·,t0), and bound b. This filter is countably generated: the punctured intervals 0<|h|<1/k, with positive integer k, form a countable neighborhood basis. F002 supplies continuity, hence a.e. strong measurability, eventually, the everywhere dominating bound, integrability of b, and pointwise convergence. Therefore ∫F(h)→∫e_t(·,t0). For 0<|h|<η, the integrability assertions of N002 give by linearity ∫F(h)=(E(t0+h)−E(t0))/h. The limit is exactly the derivative criterion for E at the interior time t0, so E'(t0)=∫e_t(x,t0) dx. This is the derivative of the actual finite energy, not just a formal integral expression. Arbitrary t0 proves N003.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M001\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"F\",\"b\",\"e_t\",\"E\",\"t0\",\"η\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"X=ℝ^n×ℝ,W=ℝ,S=Q×I,f=e_t on positive times and zero elsewhere.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean product space is topological and ℝ is seminormed.\",\"Q×I is compact, as a finite product of closed bounded boxes.\",\"N002 gives smoothness of e_t on an open neighborhood of Q×I, all of whose times are positive.\"]},{\"input\":\"M002\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"V=K=W=ℝ,S=I,c=B,f(r)=e(x,r) for fixed x∈Q, extended by zero outside positive times; p=t0,r=t0+h; L(r) is multiplication by e_t(x,r).\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Real scalar spaces satisfy all source normed and real-like structure premises.\",\"N002 supplies the classical time derivative e_t on I; its full derivative restricts to a derivative within I.\",\"F001 with the chosen B bounds |e_t(x,r)| on Q×I; the scalar multiplication map has that same operator norm.\",\"I is a real closed interval, hence convex.\",\"t0 belongs to I by η>0.\",\"0<|h|<η puts t0+h in I.\"]},{\"input\":\"M001\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℝ^n,μ=Lebesgue volume,G=ℝ,I(source index)=ℝ,l=punctured neighborhoods of 0,F=the local F,f(x)=e_t(x,t0),bound=b.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean Lebesgue space is measurable and ℝ is a real normed space.\",\"The punctured intervals of radii 1/k give the countable basis displayed in S003.\",\"F002 gives continuity of F(h) for all sufficiently small nonzero h, hence a.e. strong measurability.\",\"F002 proves the bound everywhere for all sufficiently small nonzero h.\",\"F002 proves integrability of b.\",\"F002 proves convergence for every x, stronger than almost everywhere.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R004\",\"inputs\":[\"N002\",\"M003\",\"M006\",\"M009\",\"M010\"],\"output\":\"N004\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary t0>0.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"integer in {1,…,n}\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary j∈{1,…,n}.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"C\",\"type\":\"compact subset of ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose C from N002 for t0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose its enclosing radius R>0 from N002.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"C\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"closed spatial box\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define Q=[−R,R]^n.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"g\",\"type\":\"smooth map ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set g(x)=u_t(x,t0)·u_j(j,x,t0).\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"u_t\",\"u_j\",\"j\",\"t0\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"d\",\"type\":\"map ℝ^n→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Set d(x)=∂_{x_j}g(x).\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"g\",\"j\",\"n\"]},{\"symbol\":\"ι\",\"type\":\"coordinate insertion bijection ℝ×ℝ^(n−1)→ℝ^n\",\"scope\":\"R004/argument\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Let ι(z,y) put z in coordinate j and the coordinates of y in the remaining positions in increasing order.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"j\"]},{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^(n−1)\",\"scope\":\"R004/coordinate section\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary remaining coordinates y to evaluate the one-variable section.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0 and j∈{1,…,n}; N002 gives n≥1 and the compact set C, radius R>0, and Q=[−R,R]^n. Define g(x)=u_t(x,t0)·u_j(j,x,t0) and d=∂_{x_j}g. They are smooth by N002. Apply M006 along the jth spatial coordinate line to the sections of u_t and u_j. The product rule gives d=v_j·u_j+u_t·u_jj. N002 states integrability of d and of its two summands, and also g=0 outside C. Since C⊆int Q, g vanishes on an open neighborhood of the boundary of Q, in particular at each pair of opposite j-coordinate faces.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"j\",\"t0\",\"C\",\"R\",\"Q\",\"g\",\"d\",\"u_t\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"d(x)=v_j(j,x,t0)·u_j(j,x,t0)+u_t(x,t0)·u_jj(j,x,t0). The three scalar functions in this equality are continuous, integrable, and zero outside C. The function g is smooth and zero off C⊆int Q, so it vanishes at the j-coordinate endpoints ±R for every choice of the remaining coordinates.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^n\"}],\"objects\":[\"d\",\"v_j\",\"u_j\",\"u_t\",\"u_jj\",\"j\",\"x\",\"t0\",\"C\",\"Q\",\"g\",\"R\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Define the insertion map ι. Fix arbitrary remaining coordinates y∈ℝ^(n−1). The function z↦g(ι(z,y)) is smooth on ℝ and its derivative is z↦d(ι(z,y)). The derivative is continuous on [−R,R], hence interval-integrable there. Apply M003 with endpoints −R,R: ∫_{−R}^R d(ι(z,y)) dz=g(ι(R,y))−g(ι(−R,y))=0 by F001. These sections of d vanish when |z|>R. Thus their full real integrals equal the interval integrals and are zero. The oriented interval integral here equals the Lebesgue integral on [−R,R], since −R<R and the two endpoints have measure zero. This argument holds for every y; when n=1 there are no remaining coordinates and ℝ^0 is a one-point product with total measure one.\",\"uses\":[\"M003\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"j\",\"g\",\"d\",\"ι\",\"y\",\"R\",\"Q\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"For every y0∈ℝ^(n−1), the section z↦d(ι(z,y0)) is integrable on ℝ and ∫_ℝ d(ι(z,y0)) dz=0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"y0\",\"type\":\"point of ℝ^(n−1)\"},{\"symbol\":\"z\",\"type\":\"real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"y0\",\"z\",\"d\",\"ι\",\"n\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Under the coordinate extraction inverse to ι, M009 identifies Lebesgue measure on ℝ^n with Lebesgue measure on ℝ times the product measure on ℝ^(n−1). Use k=n−1, each coordinate space ℝ, and index j−1 in Fin n; n≥1 makes n=(n−1)+1. Hence the integrable function d from F001 has the same integral and integrability as (z,y)↦d(ι(z,y)) on the product. Apply M010 to that product function; its integral is ∫_{ℝ^(n−1)}(∫_ℝ d(ι(z,y)) dz)dy=0 by F002. Equivalently this is the supplied Fubini calculation over Q, since every term is zero outside Q and all terms are absolutely integrable. In particular ∫d=0. By F001 and linearity for integrable functions, 0=∫v_j·u_j+∫u_t·u_jj. Symmetry of the real dot product and rearrangement give ∫u_j·v_j=−∫u_t·u_jj, with all integrals finite. Arbitrary t0 and j yield N004.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M009\",\"M010\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"j\",\"t0\",\"d\",\"g\",\"ι\",\"Q\",\"u_j\",\"v_j\",\"u_t\",\"u_jj\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"K=ℝ,W=ℝ^m; f and g(source) are the j-coordinate sections of u_t(·,t0) and u_j(j,·,t0).\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Euclidean ℝ^m is a real inner-product and normed space.\",\"N002 exports smoothness of u_t, giving differentiability of its spatial section.\",\"N002 exports smoothness of u_j, giving differentiability of its spatial section.\"]},{\"input\":\"M003\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"W=ℝ,f(z)=g(ι(z,y)),a=−R,b=R.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℝ is a complete real normed space.\",\"F001 and the coordinate insertion give a smooth section on all ℝ, so differentiability holds including endpoints.\",\"Its derivative d∘ι is continuous on [−R,R] by F001, hence integrable on this compact finite-measure interval.\"]},{\"input\":\"M009\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"k=n−1,A_i=ℝ for every i∈Fin n,i=j−1; the native extraction map is ι inverse.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Every coordinate has the standard real Lebesgue measure-space structure.\",\"Real Lebesgue measure is sigma-finite, by the increasing cover by bounded intervals.\",\"N002 gives n≥1 and j∈{1,…,n}, so the converted index is in Fin((n−1)+1).\"]},{\"input\":\"M010\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℝ,B=ℝ^(n−1),μ and ν their Lebesgue measures,W=ℝ,f(z,y)=d(ι(z,y)).\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"These are standard measurable product spaces with sigma-finite, hence s-finite, Lebesgue measures; the empty product for n=1 has measure one; ℝ is a real normed space.\",\"F001 gives integrability of d on ℝ^n, and M009 in S003 transports it through the volume-preserving measurable coordinate equivalence.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R005\",\"inputs\":[\"N001\",\"N002\",\"N003\",\"N004\",\"M002\"],\"output\":\"P\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"t0\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R005/pointwise energy derivative\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S001\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary t0>0 for the derivative computation.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"s\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R005/constancy\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary positive s.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"positive real number\",\"scope\":\"R005/constancy\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Fix arbitrary positive t.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[]},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"map ℝ→ℝ\",\"scope\":\"R005/constancy\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S002\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define f(r)=E(r) for r>0 and f(r)=0 for r≤0.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"E\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Fix t0>0. N002 already proves integrability of e at every positive time and supplies the definition guard for E, as well as integrability of every term in the following calculation. N003 supplies the derivative of the finite energy. Substitute the density formula from N002 and then N004 for each spatial index. By linearity and finite summation,\\nE'(t0)=∫e_t(x,t0)dx\\n=2∫u_t·u_tt dx+2∑_{j=1}^n∫u_j·v_j dx\\n=2∫u_t·u_tt dx−2∑_{j=1}^n∫u_t·u_jj dx\\n=2∫u_t·(u_tt−Δu) dx=0.\\nThe penultimate equality uses the definition Δu=∑_j u_jj and bilinearity of the dot product. The last integrand is zero at every point by N001, so its integral is zero. This retains the factor two and the energy normalization without inserting a half. Since t0 was arbitrary, E is differentiable with zero derivative at every positive time.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N002\",\"N003\",\"N004\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"t0\",\"n\",\"E\",\"e\",\"e_t\",\"u_t\",\"u_tt\",\"u_j\",\"u_jj\",\"v_j\",\"Δu\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"For every r>0, E is differentiable at r and E'(r)=0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"positive real number\"}],\"objects\":[\"E\",\"r\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"For completeness retain the supplied constancy consequence. Fix arbitrary s,t>0 and define the zero extension f of E as declared. On the closed interval with endpoints s,t, all times are positive. F001 says f is differentiable there with derivative zero; locality justifies use of the extension. Apply M002 with V=K=W=ℝ, this convex interval, c=0, and L identically zero. It gives |E(t)−E(s)|≤0·|t−s|=0, so E(t)=E(s), including the case s=t. Thus E is constant on (0,∞). Together with N002's integrability and F001 this proves P; no endpoint t=0 was used or asserted.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\",\"M002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"s\",\"t\",\"f\",\"E\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M002\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"V=K=W=ℝ,f the zero extension of E,S the unordered closed interval between s,t,c=0,L(r)=0,p=s,r(source)=t.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The scalar real spaces satisfy all normed-space and real-like field conditions.\",\"F001 gives derivative zero at every point of S, since min(s,t)>0; restrict this full derivative to S.\",\"The zero operator has norm zero, so the bound is 0≤0.\",\"A real closed interval is convex, including a singleton.\",\"s is an endpoint of S.\",\"t is an endpoint of S.\"]}]}]}}}","status":"completed"}}

